&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>[ElasticSearch]Modifying ES Mapping</title><link>https://blog.coinidea.com/en/p/elasticsearchmodifying-es-mapping/</link><pubDate>Sun, 19 Nov 2017 12:23:18 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.coinidea.com/en/p/elasticsearchmodifying-es-mapping/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Having used MySQL-like databases for so long, one day I ran into a major pitfall with ES.&lt;br&gt;
When posting the mapping, I didn&amp;rsquo;t properly distinguish between &amp;ldquo;analyzed&amp;rdquo; and &amp;ldquo;not_analyzed&amp;rdquo; for a field, which accidentally caused all data in that column to be tokenized. The dataset had approximately 150 million records.&lt;br&gt;
I naively thought I could just modify the field attributes like in MySQL. ES is based on Lucene, and there&amp;rsquo;s no other way — in simple terms, you either delete the index and re-import, or reindex. Reindexing means creating a new index and copying the data from the old index over. There are many tutorials online for this, for example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a class="link" href="http://blog.csdn.net/loveyaqin1990/article/details/77684599" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
&gt;http://blog.csdn.net/loveyaqin1990/article/details/77684599&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a class="link" href="https://www.cnblogs.com/wmx3ng/p/4112993.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
&gt;https://www.cnblogs.com/wmx3ng/p/4112993.html&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As for actual implementation code, there&amp;rsquo;s very little available online. After a long search, I only found a Python implementation. This article provides a migration implementation based on the official ES PHP SDK and bulk API.&lt;/p&gt;
